<section class="part entry draft" id="d-158">
<h3><span class="num">3 of 12 · P-158</span>Abstract members of an api</h3>
<p class="gist">The sentence on the specification's export rule now says that the rule is revised to ask what P-158 asks.</p>
<h4 class="label">The new text</h4>
<div class="entry-text diff"><p>P-158 <strong>A member that an api writes <code>abstract</code> is declared in the component that exports the api.</strong> The component's trait or object declares each such member at the api's header, with a body or without one. The compiled export check refuses a component that leaves one out, and the checker then checks that each object defines it. Why: the compiler's prelude, the team's later work, restates each such member in the component without a body, and the team's export check enforces it; that way wins over the interpreter's library, written earlier for walk, which leaves some out. The specification's export rule <del data-old="asks no declaration of an abstract member"></del> <ins>is revised to ask the same</ins> (F-294). Without the declaration, a compiled client's call of the member dies with <code>NoSuchMethodError</code> (row 816).</p></div>
<details class="old"><summary>The current text</summary><p>P-158 <strong>A member that an api writes <code>abstract</code> is declared in the component that exports the api.</strong> The component's trait or object declares each such member at the api's header, with a body or without one. The compiled export check refuses a component that leaves one out, and the checker then checks that each object defines it. Why: the compiler's prelude, the team's later work, restates each such member in the component without a body, and the team's export check enforces it; that way wins over the interpreter's library, written earlier for walk, which leaves some out. The specification's export rule asks no declaration of an abstract member (F-294). Without the declaration, a compiled client's call of the member dies with <code>NoSuchMethodError</code> (row 816).</p></details>
<div class="reasons">
<h4 class="label">The words removed and added</h4>
<ul class="change">
<li><span class="k">Removed:</span> “The specification's export rule asks no declaration of an abstract member (F-294).”</li>
<li><span class="k">Added:</span> “The specification's export rule is revised to ask the same (F-294).”</li>
</ul>
<p class="length">Length: 767 characters now, 752 drafted.</p>
<h4 class="label">What is false</h4>
<p>The sentence says that the specification's export rule asks no declaration of an abstract member. An abstract member is a method or field that an api's trait writes <code>abstract</code>, with no body. That was the rule of the Working Draft, the team's specification of February 2011.</p>
<p>Climb batch 22c revised the rule to follow P-158, in the revision form: a box at the passage, an entry in Appendix I that quotes the original text, and a decision record. A declaration in the component that satisfies the api's trait now also declares each method that the api declares abstract, at its header, with a body or without one. Appendix I, the specification's list of the revival's changes, keeps the old rule under “An abstract method of an exported trait”.</p>
<h4 class="label">What the change alters in the work</h4>
<p>Nothing built changes, and your meaning stays. The corrected sentence keeps the pointer to F-294, the fact in FACTS that holds the Working Draft's rule. You knew that rule when you answered the question on P-158. A worker who reads the specification now finds P-158's rule there.</p>
<p>The next sentence names row 816 of the gap ledger, the list of known gaps, which is now closed. That sentence gives the reason for the declaration, which still holds, so it stays as it reads.</p>
</div>
