<section class="part entry draft" id="d-58">
<h3><span class="num">4 of 12 · P-58</span>The natives' form</h3>
<p class="gist">“Every binding still names a glue class.” and its sentences on rows 796 and 309 give way to a pointer to F-32, the fact that counts the bindings that still name a glue class.</p>
<h4 class="label">The new text</h4>
<div class="entry-text diff"><p>P-58 <strong>Every native binding is a <code>builtinPrimitive</code> call naming a static helper.</strong> A native binding is a library body that is exactly <code>builtinPrimitive("…")</code>, the library's own form, and it keeps that form. Why: the language has no foreign-function interface, only a planned one, so the library's own practice decides the binding's form. Its string is to name a static Java method in <code>nativeHelpers/</code>, the compiled path's helpers, such as <code>"com.sun.fortress.nativeHelpers.simpleIntArith.intLT"</code>, in place of a glue class of walk, which the compiled path cannot use (F-32). Walk is to load the named helper through a loader beside its glue classes. At the parse, before the checker, the compiled path is to rewrite each such body into a synthetic <code>import java</code> of the helper and a call, and to refuse a string that names no helper. The bindings move onto helpers step by step, and from the step that moves a binding, the compiled checker checks it against the helper's Java signature, not all at once at the switch-over. The other form, <code>import java</code> declarations written in the library itself, is not taken. <del data-old="Every binding"></del> <ins>F-32 counts the bindings that</ins> still <del data-old="names"></del> <ins>name</ins> a glue class. <del data-old="Row 796: walk loads a binding&#x27;s string only as a glue class. Row 309: the compiled path has no counterpart of builtinPrimitive."></del></p></div>
<details class="old"><summary>The current text</summary><p>P-58 <strong>Every native binding is a <code>builtinPrimitive</code> call naming a static helper.</strong> A native binding is a library body that is exactly <code>builtinPrimitive("…")</code>, the library's own form, and it keeps that form. Why: the language has no foreign-function interface, only a planned one, so the library's own practice decides the binding's form. Its string is to name a static Java method in <code>nativeHelpers/</code>, the compiled path's helpers, such as <code>"com.sun.fortress.nativeHelpers.simpleIntArith.intLT"</code>, in place of a glue class of walk, which the compiled path cannot use (F-32). Walk is to load the named helper through a loader beside its glue classes. At the parse, before the checker, the compiled path is to rewrite each such body into a synthetic <code>import java</code> of the helper and a call, and to refuse a string that names no helper. The bindings move onto helpers step by step, and from the step that moves a binding, the compiled checker checks it against the helper's Java signature, not all at once at the switch-over. The other form, <code>import java</code> declarations written in the library itself, is not taken. Every binding still names a glue class. Row 796: walk loads a binding's string only as a glue class. Row 309: the compiled path has no counterpart of <code>builtinPrimitive</code>.</p></details>
<div class="reasons">
<h4 class="label">The words removed and added</h4>
<ul class="change">
<li><span class="k">Removed:</span> “Every binding still names a glue class. Row 796: walk loads a binding's string only as a glue class. Row 309: the compiled path has no counterpart of <code>builtinPrimitive</code>.”</li>
<li><span class="k">Added:</span> “F-32 counts the bindings that still name a glue class.”</li>
</ul>
<p class="length">Length: 1,279 characters now, 1,164 drafted.</p>
<h4 class="label">What is false</h4>
<p>A native binding is a library body that is exactly <code>builtinPrimitive("…")</code>. Its string names the Java code that does the work. A glue class is a Java class of walk, the interpreter, that such a string names. A static helper is a static Java method in <code>nativeHelpers/</code>, the compiled path's helpers.</p>
<p>“Every binding still names a glue class.” is false since climb batch 23. The one library is the interpreter's library, which both paths will use. Of its 449 live bindings, 21 now name a static helper: 19 of trait <code>ZZ32</code>'s operators and functions, and <code>Int</code>'s <code>asString</code> and <code>asExprString</code>. In the tree, 19 such strings are in <code>FortressLibrary.fss</code> and 2 in <code>FortressBuiltin.fss</code>.</p>
<p>Rows 796 and 309 of the gap ledger, the list of known gaps, are closed. Row 796 said that walk loaded a binding's string only as a glue class. Row 309 said that the compiled path had no counterpart of <code>builtinPrimitive</code>. Walk's loader and the compiled path's rewrite are now built.</p>
<h4 class="label">What the change alters in the work</h4>
<p>The three sentences state a status, which the record keeps out of an entry. Each batch of phase 4, the switch-over, changes the count, and the count has one home: F-32, a fact in FACTS, counts the bindings that still name a glue class, 428 of 449. So the three sentences give way to a pointer to F-32.</p>
<p>The pointer keeps one thing that the cut would lose. Without a word that bindings still name glue classes, the title reads as a fact about the tree. The cold read of the corrections' final texts, a check by a reader new to them, flagged that pattern in P-84's title and in P-58's.</p>
<p>Nothing in the work changes. P-58's rule stays as it reads: the bindings move onto helpers step by step, and the checker checks each one from the step that moves it. The sentences “Walk is to load ...” and “the compiled path is to rewrite ...” state what the decision asks, which is now built. They stay true and are not changed.</p>
</div>
