<section class="part entry draft" id="d-39">
<h3><span class="num">5 of 12 · P-39</span>The checker count in every gate</h3>
<p class="gist">Until the switch-over lands, every batch's gate also measures the distance, for the moved bindings that only the distance checks. This writes into the entry your answer to climb batch 23's question 1.</p>
<h4 class="label">The new text</h4>
<div class="entry-text diff"><p>P-39 <strong>Every batch's gate measures the checker count, and its total never turns the gate red.</strong> A batch is a set of tasks, each called a rung, that agents build at once and land together. Its gate runs the gate's steps on their merged tree, and then the checker count, as step 8 (<code>explorations/coordinator/climb-batch-workflow.md</code>); the gate of <code>fortress-repo</code>'s gate part has no such step. The checker count and the distance to the switch-over both count the compiled checker's errors on the interpreter's library. The count stops where the checker crashes or meets an error that it cannot get past; the distance counts every error, over all apis and components (F-53 defines both and holds their values). A rise of the count's total is reported and never fails a batch on its own. The count's stage can still turn the gate red on the faults that F-66 names. One is a checker crash that no rung of the batch listed in advance as expected, in the field <code>expectedCheckerCrash</code> of the batch's manifest, the part of the batch's script that declares each rung's task. <del data-old="The gates of batches whose work drives"></del> <ins>Until</ins> the <del data-old="distance down"></del> <ins>switch-over lands, every batch's gate</ins> also <del data-old="measure"></del> <ins>measures</ins> the distance, in a stage that only reports. Why: the <del data-old="distance&#x27;s full measurement takes far longer than"></del> <ins>count checks only</ins> the <del data-old="count, and"></del> <ins><code>FortressLibrary</code> component (F-53), so</ins> a <del data-old="batch pays for it only when"></del> <ins>native binding moved onto a helper in another component is checked by</ins> the distance <del data-old="is"></del> <ins>alone, and P-58 has each moved binding checked from</ins> the <del data-old="number"></del> <ins>step</ins> that <del data-old="it tries to move."></del> <ins>moves it.</ins></p></div>
<details class="old"><summary>The current text</summary><p>P-39 <strong>Every batch's gate measures the checker count, and its total never turns the gate red.</strong> A batch is a set of tasks, each called a rung, that agents build at once and land together. Its gate runs the gate's steps on their merged tree, and then the checker count, as step 8 (<code>explorations/coordinator/climb-batch-workflow.md</code>); the gate of <code>fortress-repo</code>'s gate part has no such step. The checker count and the distance to the switch-over both count the compiled checker's errors on the interpreter's library. The count stops where the checker crashes or meets an error that it cannot get past; the distance counts every error, over all apis and components (F-53 defines both and holds their values). A rise of the count's total is reported and never fails a batch on its own. The count's stage can still turn the gate red on the faults that F-66 names. One is a checker crash that no rung of the batch listed in advance as expected, in the field <code>expectedCheckerCrash</code> of the batch's manifest, the part of the batch's script that declares each rung's task. The gates of batches whose work drives the distance down also measure the distance, in a stage that only reports. Why: the distance's full measurement takes far longer than the count, and a batch pays for it only when the distance is the number that it tries to move.</p></details>
<div class="reasons">
<h4 class="label">The words removed and added</h4>
<ul class="change">
<li><span class="k">Removed:</span> “The gates of batches whose work drives the distance down also measure the distance, in a stage that only reports. Why: the distance's full measurement takes far longer than the count, and a batch pays for it only when the distance is the number that it tries to move.”</li>
<li><span class="k">Added:</span> “Until the switch-over lands, every batch's gate also measures the distance, in a stage that only reports. Why: the count checks only the <code>FortressLibrary</code> component (F-53), so a native binding moved onto a helper in another component is checked by the distance alone, and P-58 has each moved binding checked from the step that moves it.”</li>
</ul>
<p class="length">Length: 1,333 characters now, 1,402 drafted.</p>
<h4 class="label">What is false</h4>
<p>A batch's gate is the full check of its merged tree before it lands. The distance counts every error that the compiled checker reports on the library's core. The checker count is a quicker count, which stops where the checker crashes. The last two sentences say that only the gates of batches whose work drives the distance down measure it, since a batch pays for the measurement only then.</p>
<p>Climb batch 23's question 1 keeps the distance stage, which only reports, in every gate until the switch-over lands. You took it at its default with your go at 22:36:37 UTC on 29 October (23:36:37 your time): “And you need to run both batches.”</p>
<p>Its reason: the count checks only the <code>FortressLibrary</code> component. From climb batch 23 on, a binding moved onto a helper in another component, such as <code>FortressBuiltin</code>, is checked by the distance alone. That batch's planner measured such a case: a count of 0 and a distance of 1. So the sentences no longer describe the gates of phase 4, the switch-over, and the old reason no longer holds. It goes to the history with the old line.</p>
<h4 class="label">What the change alters in the work</h4>
<p>The work keeps what climb batch 23's gate already did: a stage of about four minutes of one core in each gate, beside the suites, until the switch-over.</p>
<p>This text rests on the review queue's item 306, which records your answer at its default. If you take item 306 the other way, the count only, P-39's two sentences stay as they read, and the stage leaves the gates.</p>
<p>One part of the default is left out: “each site that comes goes on your list”. That is how the coordinator, the session that plans and runs the batches, routes what the stage finds. It is not a rule for the gate. You may want it in.</p>
</div>
