130	<h2><span class="num">Part 1</span>Drafted corrections of POSITIONS</h2>
131	<p class="part-intro">The phase 3 exit review and climb batches 22c and 23 left sentences in POSITIONS that are now false, and reasons that are not yours. A worker drafted their corrections on 30 October, one section per entry, in the order of the review queue's items. Nothing is applied. At your yes, the record's tool writes a new text into POSITIONS, with a note that names its section, and keeps the old line in <code>POSITIONS-history.md</code>.</p>
132	<p class="part-intro">The drafts follow your rules for a correction. Each changes as little as the fact needs. A false sentence is corrected. A reason that is not yours goes to the history, and your meaning stays. No date goes into an entry.</p>
133	<p class="part-intro">Each section gives the new text, with the added words underlined and the removed words struck through, and the current text collapsed under it. Then come the words removed and added, what is false, and what the change alters in the work. Each section's Select button takes its heading, its summary and the new text. Two questions on P-42 stand in place of a text: its example, in section 1, and its word “functions”, in section 12. Each question has its own Select button, which leaves out its references.</p>
134	<p class="part-intro">The record's guide for an entry is near 600 characters. The drafts cut only what their facts need, so P-45, P-156 and P-39 stay far past it.</p>
135	<section class="part entry draft" id="d-42">
136	<h3><span class="num">1 of 12 · P-42</span>The default bound</h3>
137	<p class="gist">The last sentence goes: the compiled path no longer bounds a type parameter declared without a bound by <code>Object</code>. A question on the entry's example follows.</p>
138	<h4 class="label">The new text</h4>
139	<div class="entry-text diff"><p>P-42 <strong>A type parameter declared without a bound has the implicit bound <code>Any</code>, not <code>Object</code>.</strong> <code>Any</code> holds tuples, functions and <code>()</code>, which <code>Object</code> does not. The library instantiates its generic containers at tuple types: its arrays indexed by pairs, such as <code>(ZZ32,ZZ32)</code>, are among them. The specification states the bound <code>Any</code>, with a callout that records that the Working Draft of February 2011 said <code>Object</code>. <del data-old="The compiled path departs from it until the switch-over, which ends the departure: while it uses the compiler&#x27;s prelude, it bounds such a parameter by Object, and so refuses a generic instantiated at a tuple (row 412)."></del></p></div>
140	<details class="old"><summary>The current text</summary><p>P-42 <strong>A type parameter declared without a bound has the implicit bound <code>Any</code>, not <code>Object</code>.</strong> <code>Any</code> holds tuples, functions and <code>()</code>, which <code>Object</code> does not. The library instantiates its generic containers at tuple types: its arrays indexed by pairs, such as <code>(ZZ32,ZZ32)</code>, are among them. The specification states the bound <code>Any</code>, with a callout that records that the Working Draft of February 2011 said <code>Object</code>. The compiled path departs from it until the switch-over, which ends the departure: while it uses the compiler's prelude, it bounds such a parameter by <code>Object</code>, and so refuses a generic instantiated at a tuple (row 412).</p></details>
141	<div class="reasons">
142	<h4 class="label">The words removed and added</h4>
143	<ul class="change">
144	<li><span class="k">Removed:</span> “The compiled path departs from it until the switch-over, which ends the departure: while it uses the compiler's prelude, it bounds such a parameter by <code>Object</code>, and so refuses a generic instantiated at a tuple (row 412).”</li>
145	<li><span class="k">Added:</span> nothing.</li>
146	</ul>
147	<p class="length">Length: 639 characters now, 418 drafted.</p>
148	<h4 class="label">What is false</h4>
149	<p>The last sentence says that the compiled path bounds a type parameter declared without a bound by <code>Object</code> until the switch-over. The compiled path compiles a program to Java bytecode. The switch-over is the step at which it moves onto the interpreter's library.</p>
150	<p>The sentence is false since climb batch 22c. <code>fortress compile</code>, and the other commands that use the compiler's prelude, the compiler's own library, now bound such a parameter by <code>Any</code>, as walk, the interpreter, does. A generic instantiated at a tuple type or at a function type now compiles.</p>
151	<p>So the sentence goes, as your lean of just after 21:00 UTC on 29 October (just after 22:00 your time) has it. Nothing replaces it, since both paths now read the bound alike.</p>
152	<h4 class="label">What the change alters in the work</h4>
153	<p>Nothing built changes, and no work waits any longer for the switch-over to change the bound.</p>
154	<p>Some things still fail on the compiled path: a generic instantiated at <code>()</code>; a generic method, called with a dot, whose one parameter has a tuple type; and the prelude's <code>||</code>, <code>print</code> and <code>println</code>, which take <code>Object</code>. These are facts of the tree, which FACTS holds. They are not a departure from the bound.</p>
155	</div>
156	<section class="part entry question sub" id="d-42-example">
157	<h3><span class="num">Question on P-42 · its example</span>Does P-42 keep its example of arrays indexed by pairs?</h3>
158	<h4 class="label">The briefing</h4>
159	<p>Your lean of 29 October is that this is asked, not changed without you.</p>
160	<p>The entry's second and third sentences give the reason for the bound <code>Any</code>. The library instantiates its generic containers at tuple types, and “its arrays indexed by pairs, such as <code>(ZZ32,ZZ32)</code>, are among them”. Since P-156, the index parameter of every generic indexed type has a written bound, <code>Index</code>, and a pair of <code>ZZ32</code> is an <code>Index</code>. So an array indexed by a pair no longer rests on the implicit bound, the bound that a parameter has when none is written. The example no longer shows what the sentence claims, though the claim stays true.</p>
161	<p>Your yes of 29 September rested on the tuples, so the example is yours to change.</p>
162	<h4 class="label">The choices</h4>
163	<ol class="options">
164	<li><p><strong>Swap the example</strong>, as the corrections run of 29 October drafted it. That run drafted the corrections that landed that day. The third sentence becomes:</p><div class="entry-text diff"><p>The library instantiates its generic containers at tuple types: <del data-old="its arrays indexed by pairs, such as (ZZ32,ZZ32), are among them."></del> <ins><code>zip</code>, for one, gives a <code>ZeroIndexed[\(E,F)\]</code>, whose element type is a pair.</ins></p></div><p>The element parameter <code>E</code> of <code>Generator</code> and <code>Indexed</code> has no written bound, so this pair rests on the implicit bound.</p><p class="gives">It gives up: nothing built. The arrays leave the entry.</p></li>
165	<li><p><strong>Keep the example as it reads.</strong></p><p class="gives">It gives up: an example that holds when a reader checks it. A reader who checks it finds the bound <code>Index</code>, not the implicit bound, and may doubt the reason.</p></li>
166	</ol>
